# Kernel-log timebase (1.56.58). core/kprint.cyr is included unconditionally (agnos.cyr:57) and calls
# this at every line start; the x86 twin is in arch/x86_64/io.cyr and reads rdtsc against a boot base.
# ⛔ RETURNS 0 ON PURPOSE — DO NOT "IMPROVE" IT INTO A CNTVCT_EL0 READ WITHOUT FIXING timer.cyr FIRST.
# timer_read_freq / timer_read_count in arch/aarch64/timer.cyr (and this file own rdtsc stub) store
# and load through [sp,#0], which is FRAME PADDING, so all three return a constant 0 — an open P1,
# docs/development/issues/archived/2026-08-28-p1-audit-sweep-backlog.md:149-155. Wiring this to them would
# print fabricated timestamps that look real. 0 renders [    0.000000], which is honestly "no clock".
fn klog_uptime_us() { return 0; }

