# EXEC + PROCESS BIRTH — no ring 3 here (enter_ring3 / spawn_user_proc above refuse). The loader refuses (-1 = "not an
# executable" to shell `run`, "could not launch" to kybernet, which then drops to the recovery shell); the per-CPU env
# cells it would consume have nowhere to go. proc_create_user's tail (birth refusal, channel endowment) never runs a
# child: proc_birth_check above always answers "born" and nothing is ever armed (x86's own "nothing armed" 0).
# proc_slot_release_unborn is NOT that shape: x86's 0 means "released" (ppid / epoch / CR3 / kill / stop cleared and
# proc_count collapsed, under proctab_lock), and this stub releases nothing — so it answers x86's refusal, -1. It is
# reached only through proc_birth_check's 0, which the always-1 stub above never returns; a real aarch64 birth check
# needs a real release with it, or a refused birth leaves its slot CLAIMING with proc_count un-collapsed.
fn elf_load_from_file(path, namelen, argv_src, argv_len) { return 0 - 1; }
fn exec_env_src_set(v) { return 0; }
